How to use this software suite:

This program interfaces the code on
the Arduino Mega inside the housing of our system through a serial device,
Which is by default the device at /dev/ttyACM0. This is the port that 
the Arduino Mega chooses by default, so no extra configuration should be 
needed unless the port is already in use. If this is the case, change
the SERIAL_PORT variable to the new device. Try searching for the device
using "sudo dmesg | grep tty"

The program make file responds to the following commands:
  make clean - Cleans the build directory of csv files, deleting them
  make csv - Builds the program and creates a csvcap executable in the build directory
  make store - Moves all csv files from the build directory to the data directory

Run the program by executing the csvcap executable in the build directory
with the following command:

  ./csvcap <yourfilename>.csv

When the terminal is started, there are two sections the user will see:

1. The sensor display and settings section
- This section displays the current sensor readings and allows the user to
  change settings such as the sampling rate, the number of samples to take,
  and the type of data to capture. It has the following fields:

  TIME: The current time in milliseconds since 12:00 AM on January 1, 1970.
  DEVICE PRESSURE INSIDE: The current pressure inside the device in PSIg.
  DEVICE PRESSURE OUTSIDE: The current pressure in the hose just outside the device in PSIg.
  INTERSECTION PRESSURE: The current pressure in the housing between the two solenoid valves in PSIg.
  ACCELERATION: The current acceleration in the X, Y, and Z axes in mm/s².
  ANGULAR VELOCITY: The current angular velocity in the X, Y, and Z axes in sixteenths rad/s.

  and the following settings:

  [P]f(P):     The data transmission frequency in pressure readings per second.
  [I]f(I):     The data transmission frequency in acceleration readings per second.
  [A]f(A):     The data transmission frequency in angular frequency readings per second.
  [T]target P: The target pressure inside the device in PSIg, to two significant figures.
  [F]f(data):  The rate of writing from the data capture buffer to the CSV file in Hertz.
  [S]f(sol):   Sampling frequency for solenoid valve control in Hertz.


2. The terminal section
- The user can type in the [ Serial Console ] at the bottom of the screen by default when the terminal is selected.
  There are 14 instructions that the program accepts.
  The tilde (~) is the command prefix. All commands begin with ~. (Except for help, because how would someone know to type ~ if they need help?)
  In the notation below, the "i" character represents a number 0 to 9.

  The commands are: 

  ~Diii ~E ~Fii ~Iii ~Pii ~R ~S ~Sii ~Tiii ~X help exit ~? ~Q

  Here are their properties:

  ~E    - Toggle ON or OFF the control system and data capture system.
  ~R    - Send a reset command to all peripheral microcontrollers, control system OFF, stop data capture.
  ~Pii  - Set the pressure data transmission rate to ii samples per second.
  ~Iii  - Set the acceleration data transmission rate to ii samples per second.
  ~Fii  - Set the data capture buffer write rate to ii samples per second.
  ~Sii  - Turn ON / Set the solenoid valve control sampling rate to ** Hertz.
  ~S    - Turn OFF the solenoid valve control system.
  ~X    - Bypass the solenoid control to trap and isolate air in the balloon.
  ~Tiii - Set the target pressure of the control system to iii tenths of a PSIg (ii.i = iii /10 PSIg)
  ~Diii - Stores iii in a buffer which, at the next data capture write, is placed in the 
          last column of the csv with the newest row of data. We use this to store the diameter 
          of the balloon in tenths of an inch for later analysis, but it can store any integer value.
  help, ~? - Displays a help message to console.
  exit, ~Q - Exits the program.



The comma separated values (CSV) file will be created in the current directory with the name provided when running the program.

  The program will have the data capture system OFF upon startup, 
  and the user can toggle the control system and capture system ON or OFF using the ~E command.
  The rate of data capture can be changed using the ~Fii command.
  This rate should be less than or equal to the rate of data transmission,
  set by the ~Pii and ~Iii commands.
  The program will automatically write the data to a CSV file in the current directory.

  The format of the CSV file is as follows:

  time, internal pressure, end cap pressure, hose pressure, intersection pressure, acceleration X, Y, Z, angular velocity X, Y, Z, diameter (if entered)

  time is in milliseconds since 12:00 AM on January 1, 1970.
  pressure readings are in microPSIg. IMU data is in mm/s² and sixteenths of rad/s.
  The CSV file will be named <yourfilename>.csv, where <yourfilename> is the name you provide when you run the program.